    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Security basics</p>
    <h1>How to Check Password Strength (and What the Scores Miss)</h1>
    <p class="lead">A password strength meter gives a quick estimate, but it is not a guarantee. Length matters most, and the most common real attacks rely on patterns and reuse, which a simple score does not see.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>How the estimate works</h2>
<p>A common estimate multiplies the password length by the logarithm of the number of possible characters. Using lowercase, uppercase, digits and symbols gives 94 possible characters. A 15-character password with all four types gives about 98 bits, which is strong for most uses.</p>
<h2>What the score misses</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Common words:</strong> Password1! looks complex to a score, but attackers try common words first.</li>
<li><strong>Reuse:</strong> a strong password reused on many sites can still be stolen when one site leaks it.</li>
<li><strong>Leaked passwords:</strong> a password found in a past breach is risky, whatever its score.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Practical advice</h2>
<ul>
<li>Aim for at least 12 to 16 characters, and make each one unique.</li>
<li>Use a password manager to generate and store them.</li>
<li>Turn on two-step verification for important accounts.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/password-strength-checker.html">Password Strength Checker</a> runs in your browser. The password you type is not stored or sent anywhere.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
  </div>
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
</Base>
